# ORCLWALK: ZZT-OOP's moving half — every direction word, the four commands
# that use one, and the two movement shorthands (M16.6).
#
# Everything on Walk Hall runs the moment the board loads, so one board entry
# sets the whole hall going and the final resting positions ARE the evidence:
# a direction that resolved wrong lands its object on a different square, and
# the checkpoint names the cell. Every object carries `cycle 1`, so the world
# is phase-insensitive and declares no `phase`. The board is entered over a
# board edge (never a passage, which pauses — GAME.PAS:1348) so the arriving
# span is modelled on both sides.
#
#   row 5   /n /north /s /south, and /opp n as a south that was written north
#   row 9   /e /east /w /west, and /cw n /ccw n as an east and a west
#   row 17  #try refused falls through to the rest of its line (try1 -> T)
#           while #try taken skips it (try2 keeps its glyph and moves one west);
#           #go retries a blocked move forever (go2 never reaches #char H) but
#           steps and continues when it can (go1 -> two north, then G);
#           /w and ?w against the same wall: / retries forever, ? gives up and
#           runs the next line (fw keeps its glyph, qw -> X);
#           #walk w then `#if blocked flow` reads the step back out (flow -> F);
#           `blocked i` and `blocked idle` are the object's own square (idl -> J)
#   row 21  a lever the player pulls from the seeker's own row, so
#           CalcDirectionSeek (GAME.PAS:1488) takes its `target.Y = y` branch
#           and the seek is RNG-free: three /seek steps west
#   row 23  a standing `step east` move order jammed against a wall sends
#           itself THUD, which stops it (thud -> T at 24,23)
#
# The four random directions are the one thing an exact-cell comparison cannot
# pin draw by draw: vanilla's RandSeed is seeded from its own boot clock and is
# not this engine's. What IS comparable is the SET each draw comes from, and
# that is what the eight chambers at the top right assert, on both sides, for
# thousands of draws:
#   *_a sits in a chamber where every legal outcome is walled and loops on
#       `#if not blocked DIR bad` — so a draw outside the set turns it into B
#   *_b sits in open ground and loops on `#if blocked DIR bad` — so a draw that
#       came back as the object's own square (or any wall) turns it into B
# rnd's chamber walls the four orthogonals and leaves the diagonals open, which
# is what makes "orthogonal" the assertion rather than "not nowhere"; rndns
# walls north and south, rndne walls north and east, and rndp n walls east and
# west, which are exactly the perpendiculars of north. Both objects keep their
# starting glyph for the whole run or the checkpoint names the cell.
world ORCLWALK
seed 0
boot 240
capture title
play
# Walk Hub -> Walk Hall across the east edge; the hall starts working at once.
move right
move right
move right
move right
move right
capture arrive-hall
settle 20
capture movers-settled
# East along the arrival row, then down column 11 to row 21, so the pull
# leaves the player in the seeker's row. (Column 1 is solid below the arrival
# square: the west border is punched at row 13 only.)
move right
move right
move right
move right
move right
move right
move right
move right
move right
move right
move down
move down
move down
move down
move down
move down
move down
move down
capture seek-before
move right
settle 20
capture seek-after
